# Normalize Python sources to LF (FIX-242 R0 P3-2).
#
# Evidence (2026-08-07, `git ls-files --eol`): the repository index is
# already 100% LF (556/556 files i/lf); core.autocrlf=true leaves mixed
# working-tree line endings on Windows (a snapshot counted 28 .py files
# with CRLF/mixed endings; non-.py files vary over time). Locking *.py to
# eol=lf makes working-tree line endings deterministic for Python sources
# without renormalizing any committed content (zero commit diff — the
# index stays LF).
*.py text eol=lf
# Lock JSON sources to LF too (FIX-250): without this, core.autocrlf=true
# leaves core/releases/*.json as CRLF stat noise (git reports "M" with zero
# content diff). The index is already LF — renormalizing is zero commit diff.
*.json text eol=lf
# FIX-451: governance hooks must stay LF in the working tree. A fresh
# Windows clone/worktree under core.autocrlf=true materializes the four
# infra/hooks files as CRLF, which both breaks git-bash hook execution and
# starves the `$`-anchored version-projection patterns (count 0 -> every
# projection face BLOCKED). The projection ENGINE tolerates CRLF matches
# (release/projection.py FIX-451), and this anchor removes the CRLF source
# itself for new checkouts. The index is already 100% LF (git ls-files
# --eol: i/lf x4), so no renormalization is needed — and none is performed:
# `git add --renormalize` is deliberately NOT run because it would stage the
# FIX-445 work-in-progress infra/hooks/commit-msg (preserved untouched).
skills/software-project-governance/infra/hooks/* text eol=lf
